comments[10]{created_at,author,text}:
  "2026-10-27T22:32:59.271Z","harness:codex","Duplicate check: complete strict3143-item corpus, refreshed open/in-progress lists, field schema extension recovery search, and full live closed pm-2jtbl8/pm-field-key-recovery metadata and comments. Those delivered owners cover built-in reserved keys and rejected-key identity, not configured project-schema flag routing. Intake only, left open and unclaimed."
  "2026-11-01T21:06:25.272Z","harness:claude-code","Implementation: parseRegisteredItemFieldAssignments takes an optional project-schema resolver (createSchemaFieldRecoveryResolver in src/sdk/lifecycle/core-field-recovery.ts, wired by both create and update). A --field key the project schema declares (by key or metadata_key, through the registry's own normalizer) is refused with project_schema_field_requires_declared_flag and changed:false. The refusal carries recovery.suggested_flags with the declared flag, next steps naming the CLI flag, the SDK option (the camelCase name Commander derives from that flag, which the SDK also reads) and pm schema show-field. A key declared for other commands only says so. Unknown keys keep extension_item_field_unknown and reserved core keys keep core_item_field_requires_dedicated_flag.\n\nCLI executability: src/cli/runtime/field-route-retry.ts rewrites exactly one matching --field key=value (or --field=key=value) token in the caller's own invocation into --<flag> value and attaches suggested_retry and suggested_retry_args. Duplicate assignments, assignments after --, a dangling --field and bare keys keep the producer recovery rather than guess.\n\nProof: the update-command spec refuses through the SDK with byte-identical item and history files, then executes the advertised SDK route (reviewUrl) successfully. Resolver/parser and retry-repair specs cover every branch.\n"
  "2026-11-01T21:46:37.369Z","harness:claude-code","Codex review round 1 on PR #1649: the advertised SDK option for a dotted cli_flag (review.url) was the dotted spelling, which readRuntimeFieldOptionValue never reads. runtimeFieldSdkOptionName (core/schema/runtime-field-values.ts) now derives it with the same normalizer the reader uses (reviewUrl), with a regression case in extensions-item-fields.spec.ts.\n"
  "2026-11-01T22:35:05.508Z","harness:claude-code","Codex review round 2 on PR #1649: with --review-url and --review.url both declared, the advertised SDK option (reviewUrl) would set both fields. runtimeFieldSdkOptionName now returns the first option name the reader accepts for this field that no other declared field accepts (camelCase of the flag preferred, then key-derived), and the hint is omitted when every name is shared. The spec runs collectRuntimeUpdateFieldValues with each advertised option and asserts that exactly the intended field is set.\n"
  "2026-11-01T23:05:41.411Z","harness:claude-code","CodeRabbit full review on PR #1649 (head f7e12f69b): when several schema declarations share a metadata_key, the resolver now routes through one whose commands include the current command (create-only and update-only declarations both sharing triage resolve to the update one for update). docs/EXTENSIONS.md now states the lookup order: extension declarations are matched first, and only names no active extension declares reach the schema routing.\n"
  "2026-11-01T23:44:16.003Z","harness:claude-code","Codex review round 3 on PR #1649: two findings, both fixed.\n- The advertised CLI flag could be as ambiguous as the SDK option: --x-y parses to xY, which another field's x.y flag also reads.\n- A schema field with cli_flag title produced an SDK hint (title) that also set the core title.\nruntimeFieldCliFlagRoute now picks the declared flag or an alias that is not a host flag and whose parsed name no other field and no host option reads. runtimeFieldSdkOptionName also excludes host option names: update/create option contracts plus global flags. A route is advertised only when it sets the field alone; otherwise the refusal says to give the field a distinct cli_flag, and no executable retry is offered.\n"
  "2026-11-02T00:01:12.339Z","harness:claude-code","CodeRabbit review of 6f3d87ab7 on PR #1649: two findings, both fixed.\n- Commander stores a dotted flag under its literal key (--review.url parses to options[\"review.url\"]), which the runtime reader never checked, so such a flag silently set nothing. This was a pre-existing reader defect that the new routing could advertise. resolveCandidateOptionKeys now also reads Commander's real key for the declared flag and each alias, and the CLI route judges ambiguity by that key.\n- Option ambiguity is now computed by definition identity rather than metadata_key, so two declarations sharing a storage key still count as distinct readers.\n"
  "2026-11-02T00:37:51.260Z","harness:claude-code","Codex review round 4 on PR #1649: three findings, all fixed.\n- Negating flags: Commander files --no-title values under title. commanderOptionAttribute now strips no-, so the route and the host reservation see the real key. The runtime reader deliberately does not read a negated spelling, since that key belongs to another option.\n- Incomplete host reservation: options registered directly in register-mutation (force, clear-tests, replace-tests and others) were missing. The reservation now uses the command's full flag contract (resolveSubcommandFlagContractsForCommand) plus global flags, registration targets and the SDK-only update keys (documentAnnotationSnapshot, runtimeFieldCommands, typed against UpdateCommandOptions).\n- Ambiguity scope: competing fields are limited to those active for the command, so a create-only field no longer suppresses a valid update route.\n"
  "2026-11-02T01:12:34.361Z","harness:claude-code","Codex review round 5 on PR #1649: two findings, both fixed.\n- A negated spelling (cli_flag no-review) could be advertised although the reader never reads its value, which Commander files under review, so following it did nothing. Negating spellings are no longer routes.\n- A short alias (-r) chosen because the primary flag is host-owned was rendered ---r. runtimeFieldFlagToken keeps a short alias's single dash, commanderOptionAttribute strips leading dashes before computing the key (-r maps to r), and the host reservation now includes the command's short flags.\n"
  "2026-11-02T01:47:59.395Z","harness:claude-code","Codex review round 6 on PR #1649: two findings, both fixed.\n- Host short flags live in contract.short, not aliases, so -t (--title) was not reserved and could be advertised. The host reservation now includes every contract's short flag.\n- Reader precedence: readRuntimeFieldOptionValue returns the first candidate key present, so a route whose key the reader checks only after a key that a host option or another field's flag can set (alias heading after title, or review.url after reviewUrl) is shadowed whenever both appear in one invocation. CLI routes and SDK hints now require that no earlier-read key of the field is host- or field-settable (isUnshadowedOptionKey); otherwise nothing executable is advertised.\n"
